questions.prompt:
(Textbook 6.5, HW6) The textbook `BinaryHeap` stores `currentSize` and a `vector<Comparable> array`, with the root at `array[1]` and `array[0]` unused. Write `insert` so that a **copy of the inserted item is placed in position 0**, which makes the percolate-up loop stop at the root without an explicit `hole > 1` test. Also give the version that does *not* use position 0, and say which is used by the textbook.

```cpp
template <typename Comparable>
void BinaryHeap<Comparable>::insert( const Comparable & x )
{
    // write code here
}
```

questions.answer:
```cpp
template <typename Comparable>
void BinaryHeap<Comparable>::insert( const Comparable & x )
{
    if( currentSize == array.size( ) - 1 )
        array.resize( array.size( ) * 2 );

    // Percolate up
    int hole = ++currentSize;
    array[ 0 ] = x;                              // sentinel copy of x in position 0
    for( ; x < array[ hole / 2 ]; hole /= 2 )    // when hole == 1, hole/2 == 0 and x < array[0] (= x) is false: the loop stops
        array[ hole ] = array[ hole / 2 ];       // slide the parent down into the hole
    array[ hole ] = x;                           // (the textbook uses std::move( array[ 0 ] ))
}
```
Why it terminates at the root: `array[0]` holds x itself, so `x < array[0]` is false; the loop can never run past the root. The version **without** position 0 needs the extra test:

```cpp
    for( ; hole > 1 && x < array[ hole / 2 ]; hole /= 2 )
        array[ hole ] = array[ hole / 2 ];
    array[ hole ] = x;
```
The textbook's Fig 6.8 already uses the position-0 version (that is why index 0 is "left open"). Both are O(log N) worst case and use d + 1 assignments (not 3d, as swaps would) when the item rises d levels.

questions.prompt:
(Textbook 6.10a, HW6) Give an algorithm that finds (prints) **all nodes less than some value X** in a binary min-heap stored in the textbook array layout (root at `array[1]`, `currentSize` elements). It must run in O(K), where K is the number of nodes it outputs. Write it as a member function and prove the bound.

```cpp
// Prints every element of the min-heap that is < x. Called as printLessThan( 1, x ).
void printLessThan( int i, const Comparable & x ) const
{
    // write code here
}
```

questions.answer:
Do a preorder traversal from the root that **does not descend into a subtree whose root is >= X**: by heap order everything below it is >= that root, hence >= X.

```cpp
void printLessThan( int i, const Comparable & x ) const
{
    if( i > currentSize || !( array[ i ] < x ) )   // no such node, or array[i] >= x: prune the whole subtree
        return;
    std::cout << array[ i ] << ' ';
    printLessThan( 2 * i, x );
    printLessThan( 2 * i + 1, x );
}
```
**Analysis.** Every call either prints a node (it is < x, there are K of them) or returns immediately. A call that returns immediately is the root call or a child of a node that was printed; each printed node makes exactly 2 calls, so there are at most 2K + 1 calls, each O(1): total **O(K)**, independent of N. (A node that is >= x is compared once and its subtree is never visited.)

**Idea:** keep two heaps: `lo`, a **max**-heap holding the smaller half, and `hi`, a **min**-heap holding the larger half, with every element of `lo` <= every element of `hi` and sizes balanced (`lo.size() == hi.size()` or `lo.size() == hi.size() + 1`). The median is then `lo.top()` (odd count) or the average of the two tops (even count).

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>

class MedianFinder {
    std::priority_queue<int> lo;                                       // max-heap: smaller half
    std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> hi;  // min-heap: larger half
public:
    void add( int x ) {
        if( lo.empty( ) || x <= lo.top( ) ) lo.push( x );              // O(log n)
        else                                hi.push( x );              // O(log n)
        if( lo.size( ) > hi.size( ) + 1 )  { hi.push( lo.top( ) ); lo.pop( ); }   // rebalance: one move, O(log n)
        else if( hi.size( ) > lo.size( ) ) { lo.push( hi.top( ) ); hi.pop( ); }
    }
    double median( ) const {                                           // precondition: at least one element
        if( lo.size( ) > hi.size( ) ) return lo.top( );                // odd count
        return ( lo.top( ) + hi.top( ) ) / 2.0;                        // even count
    }
};
```
**Analysis.** `add`: one push (O(log n)) plus at most one pop and one push for rebalancing (O(log n) each), so **O(log n)** worst case; `median`: two `top()` calls, **O(1)**. For a stream of n numbers: n x O(log n) = **O(n log n)** total time (O(1) per median query), O(n) space.
A sorted `vector` with binary search finds the spot in O(log n) but insertion shifts elements: O(n) per add, **O(n^2)** for the stream; re-sorting each time is O(n log n) per add. Two heaps win.

questions.prompt:
You have k sorted `vector<int>`s with N elements in total. Design an algorithm using `priority_queue` that produces one sorted vector, write the function, and analyze its running time and extra space in terms of N and k. (Assume no list is huge relative to the others; state your bound in terms of N and k.)

```cpp
std::vector<int> mergeK( const std::vector<std::vector<int>> & lists )
{
    // write code here
}
```

questions.answer:
Keep a **min**-heap holding the current front element of every non-empty list, together with which list it came from and its position. Repeatedly pop the smallest, append it to the output, and push the next element of the same list.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <vector>
#include <tuple>
#include <functional>

std::vector<int> mergeK( const std::vector<std::vector<int>> & lists )
{
    using Item = std::tuple<int, int, int>;            // (value, list index, position in that list)
    std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<Item>> pq;   // min-heap on value
    for( int i = 0; i < (int) lists.size( ); ++i )
        if( !lists[ i ].empty( ) )
            pq.push( Item( lists[ i ][ 0 ], i, 0 ) );  // at most k pushes

    std::vector<int> out;
    while( !pq.empty( ) )
    {
        Item t = pq.top( ); pq.pop( );                 // O(log k)
        int v = std::get<0>( t ), i = std::get<1>( t ), j = std::get<2>( t );
        out.push_back( v );                            // amortized O(1)
        if( j + 1 < (int) lists[ i ].size( ) )
            pq.push( Item( lists[ i ][ j + 1 ], i, j + 1 ) );   // O(log k)
    }
    return out;
}
```
**Analysis.** The heap never holds more than k items (one per list). Initial pushes: k x O(log k) = O(k log k) (or O(k) with buildHeap). The loop runs N times; each iteration does one pop O(log k), one push O(log k), one push_back amortized O(1): **O(N log k)**. Total **O(N log k + k log k) = O(N log k)** (each non-empty list contributes at least one element, so k <= N). Extra space: **O(k)** for the heap plus the O(N) output. (Compare: concatenating and sorting is O(N log N); merging lists pairwise one after another is O(N k).)
